<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_nav_profile — bottom-nav Profile tab icon
    ------------------------------------------------------------
    Referenced by menu_bottom_nav.xml:
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_nav_profile"

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       All paths fill with #FFFFFFFF. BottomNavigationView
       applies the selected/unselected tint from the theme
       (colorPrimary when selected, colorOnSurfaceVariant
       when not). Do NOT set a brand color here.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches ic_nav_chats.xml and ic_nav_people.xml.
       All three nav icons must be the same viewport and
       stroke weight, or the nav bar looks visually
       inconsistent.

    3. SINGLE FIGURE INSIDE A CIRCLE.
       The circle is what distinguishes this icon from
       ic_nav_people (two figures, no circle). Together
       these read as:
         - ic_nav_people  → "other people" (plural)
         - ic_nav_profile → "me" (singular, framed)
       This is the same visual language used by WhatsApp,
       Telegram, Signal, Instagram, and every other app
       with both a "People" and a "Profile" destination.
       Without the circle, the single-figure icon would be
       nearly indistinguishable from one of the two figures
       in ic_nav_people at 24dp — a real usability problem.

    4. OUTLINED STROKE WEIGHT (~2 units).
       Same stroke weight as the two sibling icons. Do not
       mix filled and outlined styles in the nav bar.

    5. THREE OVERLAPPING CONTOURS, THREE PATHS.
       - Circle outline (head-of-circle metaphor container):
         outer + inner circle with evenOdd → ring
       - Head silhouette: solid-filled inner head shape
         (a small circle positioned near the top-center of
         the outer circle's interior)
       - Shoulders silhouette: solid half-circle extending
         from the bottom half of the outer circle's interior

       Why solid head and shoulders (not outlined):
         At 24dp, three nested outlines (circle outline,
         head outline, shoulder outline) produce visual
         clutter — the head and shoulders would read as
         "another circle inside a circle" and lose their
         person-silhouette meaning. The solid head and
         solid shoulders against the outlined container
         circle is the standard Material construction for
         "account_circle" and "person_in_circle" icons,
         and it reads instantly at small sizes.

    6. NO android:autoMirrored.
       The composition is fully symmetric. Mirroring under
       RTL produces no visible change.

    Rendering result at 24dp:
      A circle outline (like a coin or badge) containing
      a simple person silhouette (round head + shoulder
      arch). Instant "this is my account" recognition.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!-- ============================================================
         OUTER CIRCLE (the "avatar frame")
         ------------------------------------------------------------
         Drawn as an evenOdd ring: outer circle minus inner
         circle, producing a hollow outline ~1.5 units thick.
         The inner circle defines the visual boundary that
         the head and shoulders are drawn INSIDE — this is
         why the head and shoulder paths below stay in the
         y-range 5–18 and x-range 5–19.

         Outer circle: center (12,12), radius 10 → spans (2,2)
                       to (22,22)
         Inner circle: center (12,12), radius 9 → spans (3,3)
                       to (21,21)
         Stroke:       1 unit thick (outer - inner)

         A 1-unit stroke is slightly thinner than the 2-unit
         stroke of the sibling nav icons, but because this
         outline is circular (a single continuous curve) the
         visual weight reads as equivalent — the eye perceives
         curvature as more weight than straight lines. Using
         a 2-unit stroke here would make the circle look heavy
         and would compete with the head/shoulder silhouettes
         inside it.
         ============================================================ -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
        android:pathData="M2,12 A10,10 0 1,0 22,12 A10,10 0 1,0 2,12 Z M3,12 A9,9 0 1,1 21,12 A9,9 0 1,1 3,12 Z" />

    <!-- ============================================================
         HEAD SILHOUETTE (solid filled circle)
         ------------------------------------------------------------
         Center (12, 9.5), radius 2.8 → spans (9.2, 6.7) to
         (14.8, 12.3).

         Positioned in the upper portion of the outer circle,
         leaving a small gap (~1.5 units) between the top of
         the head and the circle outline. This gap is what
         makes the head read as "inside the frame" rather
         than "breaking out of the frame."

         Solid fill (nonZero, default) — no inner contour,
         no evenOdd. The head is a solid disk, not a ring.
         See file header constraint #5 for why.
         ============================================================ -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M12,6.7 A2.8,2.8 0 1,0 12,12.3 A2.8,2.8 0 1,0 12,6.7 Z" />

    <!-- ============================================================
         SHOULDER SILHOUETTE (solid filled arch)
         ------------------------------------------------------------
         A semicircle / arch starting at (7, 17.5), curving
         up to a flat top at y=12.5 and back down to (17, 17.5).
         The arch represents the person's shoulders and upper
         torso.

         Traced:
           M 7,17.5                  left shoulder base
           Q 7,12.5 12,12.5          up to top-center (curve)
           Q 17,12.5 17,17.5         down to right shoulder
           L 7,17.5                  close (flat bottom edge)
           Z

         The flat bottom edge at y=17.5 is intentional — it
         visually anchors the shoulders "inside" the circle.
         If the shoulders extended below the circle's inner
         edge (y=21), they would break out of the frame. The
         4-unit bottom gap (17.5 → 21) is deliberate breathing
         room.

         Solid fill (nonZero, default). See file header
         constraint #5.
         ============================================================ -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M7,17.5 Q7,12.5 12,12.5 Q17,12.5 17,17.5 Z" />

</vector>
